----
== YAML has an Anchor/Alias problem

  vars: &vars
    app: &app nginx
    defaults: &defaults
      replicas: &replicas 3
      namespace: dev

  all: *vars
  app: *app
  replicas: *replicas
  deployment:
    <<: *defaults
    namespace: prod

* We don't want `vars` in the final result

$ yaml -J anchors.yaml

----
== Using Multiple YAML Documents Doesn't Help

  ---
  vars: &vars
    app: &app nginx
    defaults: &defaults
      replicas: &replicas 3
      namespace: dev
  ---
  all: *vars
  app: *app
  replicas: *replicas
  deployment:
    <<: *defaults
    namespace: prod

* %The YAML Spec forbids cross document aliases (references)%

+* That's Dumb! Let's break the rules!

* The new go-yaml v4 AliasData plugin lets us do this and more!

----
== What if YAML could import more YAML?

* Put our variable data in a separate `vars.yaml` file:

    app: nginx
    defaults:
      replicas: 3
      namespace: dev

* Now let's import it into our main YAML file

----
== Function Calls using YAML Tags

* Our main YAML file is `input.yaml`:

    %!import% `vars`: vars.yaml  # Create `vars` "anchor"
    all: `*vars`      # Alias to `vars` anchor
    app: `*vars.app`  # .path selection works!
    replicas: `*vars.defaults.replicas`
    deployment:
      <<: `*vars.defaults`  # Merge defaults
      namespace: @prod@

* We can make this YAML work with 2 new ^go-yaml plugins^:
  * go.yaml.in/yaml/v4/plugin/@aliasdata@
  * go.yaml.in/yaml/v4/plugin/@functiontag@

> vim input.yaml vars.yaml -O
> go run main.go
> vim main.go

----
== Make ^Custom YAML DSLs^ with @Implicit Tagging@

* You can set up any tag for any function…
* You can do this without using tags at all!

  You could change:
    %!import% `vars`: vars.yaml
  To something like this:
    %import% `vars`: vars.yaml
  Or maybe:
    ^<<&vars^: vars.yaml

* Craft your own custom YAML DSLs!

* The ^YAML-Rules^ plugin lets you determine @Tag Resolution@

+* I think I need to show you how YAML really works!

----
== The YAML %Dump%/%Load% Stack (How a YAML really works)
== https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#31-processes

              %Loader%             Data State              %Dumper%
         ===========================================================
                                (YAML Input)
              Reader                                     Writer
                           (YAML Character Stream)
             Scanner
                             (YAML Token Stream)
              `Parser`                                     Emitter
                             ^(YAML Event Stream)^
            `Composer`                                     Serializer
                               (YAML Node Tree)
            `Resolver`                                     Desolver
                        ^(Tagged Representation Graph)^
         `Constructor`                                     Representer
                            (Native Data Objects)

----
== Tag Resolution

$ yaml --debug <<<'a: [b, 3, true]'

* YAML adds a tag to every untagged node
* This is called Tag Resolution
* The Constructor phase uses the tag to look up a function (always)

+* ^The internal state after tag resolution:^

    `!!map`
    `!!str` "a": `!!seq` [`!!str` "b", `!!int` "3", `!!bool` "true"]

* Implicitly Tagged YAML:
$ yaml -J <<<'a: [b, 3, true]'
* Explicitly Tagged:
$ yaml -J <<<'!!map {!!str "a": !!seq [!!str "b", !!int "3", !!bool "true"]}'

* We just configured our own Tag and Constructor function to make a DSL!

----
== go-yaml v4 comes with a ^New CLI^ called '`go-yaml`'

$ cat vars.yaml

* Convert YAML
$ go-yaml -J vars.yaml

* Inspect internal Loader states:
$ go-yaml -t vars.yaml
$ go-yaml -e vars.yaml
$ go-yaml -N vars.yaml
* Great for debugging YAML issues
  * Please, ^Use the CLI to report bugs^

* Use plugins. Configure go-yaml with more YAML!
$ vim options.yaml
$ go-yaml -y --config=options.yaml input.yaml

----
== What you just saw…

* ^YAML's existing syntax doing new things^
  * Using optional plugins with go-yaml v4

+* %Function-Tag plugin% connects tags to Go functions
  * We chose `!import` and the function it calls

* %Alias-Data plugin% makes imported values available through aliases
  * Dotted paths select values; merges combine defaults and overrides

* %YAML-Rules plugin% can assign tags implicitly
  * Turn ordinary YAML into your own DSL

+* The same plugins work through Go and %new go-yaml CLI%
  * Configure them in Go code or in YAML

* @Your application decides what YAML means@

+* Now let's back up and see how we got here!

